## 26. Doppler Shift (Sound Waves)

This example demonstrates the **Doppler shift** of sound waves due to the motion of the source.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  f' = f \left( \frac{v \pm v_s}{v \mp v_o} \right)
  \]
- **Description**: The frequency \( f' \) heard by an observer depends on the velocity of the source \( v_s \) and the observer \( v_o \) relative to the sound speed \( v \).

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- Frequency of source \( f = 500 \, \text{Hz} \)
- Speed of sound \( v = 343 \, \text{m/s} \)
- Speed of source \( v_s = 10 \, \text{m/s} \)

\[
f' = 500 \left( \frac{343 + 10}{343} \right) = 500 \times 1.029 = 514.5 \, \text{Hz}
\]

- **Final Frequency**: \( 514.5 \, \text{Hz} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 1.0 \, \text{Hz} \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 0.2 \% \)

