## 28. Kepler’s Third Law (Orbital Period)

This example demonstrates **Kepler’s third law** for calculating the orbital period of a satellite.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  T^2 = \frac{4\pi^2}{GM} \cdot r^3
  \]
- **Description**: The orbital period \( T \) depends on the mass \( M \) of the central object and the orbital radius \( r \).

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- Gravitational constant \( G = 6.67430 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2} \)
- Mass of the Sun \( M = 1.989 \times 10^{30} \, \text{kg} \)
- Orbital radius \( r = 1.496 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m} \) (1 AU)

\[
T^2 = \frac{4\pi^2 (1.496 \times 10^{11})^3}{(6.67430 \times 10^{-11})(1.989 \times 10^{30})} = 3.15576 \times 10^7 \, \text{s} = 365.25 \, \text{days}
\]

- **Final Period**: \( 365.25 \, \text{days} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 0.01 \, \text{days} \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 0.002 \% \)

